#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445202} :activity/id #uuid "632f0c8c-4357-5396-aad3-4401d3471d8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f22169-b8a2-5fb3-aca1-3e5599415ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876} {:answer/id #uuid "abbaa7f2-5259-5b5a-bebd-07f255b181e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367877} {:answer/id #uuid "f674340e-9bc4-5748-9997-f0404db95ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367878} {:answer/id #uuid "383240c1-34bf-5387-adaf-b9355e4cf27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367879} {:answer/id #uuid "0e483432-9f14-537e-b0dd-61a175cc1645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876} :answer-field/id #uuid "be669623-0526-55a8-9e58-06217378cfec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f0502ee-0a83-5915-919b-cb8a0333bfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163775" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-3<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-9<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "40399da2-21fd-5f08-bac2-f30a1d0cf8b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118339a7-669b-5081-8175-449ccce667f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445192} {:answer/id #uuid "80185868-d8d6-5a9a-9b8a-83d741afd936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445193} {:answer/id #uuid "b251a48a-e4be-5932-a217-f573cfba9f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445194} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b3e9e9-2073-5961-8289-000100206e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445195} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196} :answer-field/id #uuid "261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" :question/math-academy-id "q-164157" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)"} :step/id #uuid "781a150f-a171-5196-8aca-b895a99d8faa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321b323f-56e0-55fb-b404-b19f30c4f96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-1.2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282139} {:answer/id #uuid "cfae193b-da7f-5e1b-bf9e-4ef2f1177187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283501} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e29d66-dcd3-589d-8fbe-6a8511fdfa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283513} {:answer/id #uuid "f4af8296-0c7f-540f-a7a3-bb0a43f520a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283827} {:answer/id #uuid "fadd78d8-5eef-5833-8275-ca89bd91ab2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283513} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ed0cd9-02bd-5986-85dc-65dabf690efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25f6d53a-4346-557b-a7bc-14685aa9ba47" :question/math-academy-id "q-164169" :question/problem "![](images/86/867fa59781c1732536e984398478996150c3d6b715c9d216bb82a35e0caf27e0.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,1),$ so the global minimum point is $(1,1,-2).$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2c084b2697ada9a957eec3629ac71aea150563a3af37b4f5d0b25e1921559a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7aa4c459-af9e-558f-a889-0dc28bf20ece"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488efa2b-10b1-5ebf-8034-0b0da5c882dc" :answer/t